# services-workspace corpus notes

Two members, one direction of dependency: `contracts/` declares shapes and
publishes nothing; `vehicle/` publishes and declares no shape of its own. That
split is the point — an `interface` is identity-less and location-less (ridl
§14.0), so deciding *who* publishes it is a separate act, and here it is taken
in a separate package.

What the entry exercises that `veh-cluster` does not:

- **A cross-package service.** `service fleet.vehicle.doors.front : DoorControl`
  names an interface that lives in the sibling member. The checker must resolve
  the imported name before it can tell the service names an interface at all;
  an unresolved name would surface as RIDL-141 ("names a type that is not an
  interface"), so a clean compile here is the evidence that the cross-package
  path works.
- **One shape, several services.** `doors.front` and `doors.rear` are two
  system-wide addresses realizing the identical contract — the redundancy /
  variant case §14.5 calls out.
- **An inline shape that owns none of its vocabulary.** Every payload,
  parameter and return type in `fleet.vehicle.mirrors` is a cross-package
  reference. This is the inline-shape blind spot crossed with the cross-package
  reference path: both the IR and the generated TypeScript must canonicalize
  the references from inside `Service.shape`, not only from inside
  `Package.interfaces`.
- **Contracts inside an inline shape**, including an `ensure` over `result`.

The entry compiles clean, so its IR, Rust and TypeScript snapshots are golden.
